**Read:** [Calibration of aerosol instruments — Chen, Fletcher and Cheng](../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP), sections 21.2–21.4. Revisit [Begin with a question and specify the measurand](../../../inquiry/metrology/foundations/Question-and-measurand.lingua#r_7ZNCJFEGXNJSGJJJKGYH6MGFE6), [A specimen, a sampling target, and a result about a population](../../../inquiry/metrology/foundations/Sampling-and-representativeness.lingua#r_0F0VEC42GPQPRZM0S7MXF7GAEH) and [Air samples and surface samples have different denominators](../foundations/Air-samples-and-surface-samples-have-different-denominators.lingua#r_54C0X26G9K41P87TQDZ279SXKN).

Specify the particle population, size convention, location, time interval and gas conditions. A local airborne count, a surface deposition rate and the number of defects on completed parts are different measurands. Connecting them requires transport and process evidence.

Represent the source population by number concentration per logarithmic diameter interval, n(x,t), with x = ln(d/d₀) and a stated reference diameter d₀. A schematic expected count is λ = ∫ Q(t)∫ n(x,t)A(x,t)P(x,t)η(x,t) dx dt + λ_b. Here Q is inlet volume flow, A is aspiration concentration ratio, P is downstream penetration, η is detection probability, and λ_b is expected background. This factorization assumes those stages can be represented conditionally without unmodelled particle transformation or duplication.

Aspiration can enrich a sampled population, so A is not always bounded by one. Penetration and detection probabilities are bounded by one under the stated single-particle interpretation. Size migration or fragmentation requires a response kernel rather than one scalar multiplier.

**Work:** Reduce the model to a stationary single-size sample and identify which factors a calibration actually includes. Then draw two different source populations producing the same λ. Explain what additional observation would distinguish them. Define a measured operating state before describing an entire room or production period as characterized.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
